# Ribbons (side quest, docs/story/side_quests.md): Daciana the ribbon girl writes down everyone in Vallaki who won't
# wear a festival ribbon, and leaves the list under the lamp on Main Street at dusk. Watchman Dobre collects it and
# sells it three times: to the Baron, who fills the stocks; to Lady Wachter, whose book club visits whoever's in them;
# and to Arrigal's riders, for the castle. Reached from Daciana (townsfolk:ribbon_refuse), Anton in the stocks
# (townsfolk:grumbler_now) or Master Costel. Every line here is unvoiced for now.

# --- Daciana, by day in the square (townsfolk:ribbon_girl jumps here) ---

~ daciana
if flag.ribbons_resolved
-> daciana_after
endif
if flag.forged_collected and not flag.dobre_arrested and not flag.dobre_beaten
-> sting
endif
if flag.ribbons_list_known
Daciana: The lamp on Main Street. Every dusk. Please don't make me watch.
-> END
endif
Daciana: A ribbon? They're free. They're for the sun.
* Who wants to know, Daciana? Who reads what you write down? -> who
* [if not flag.ribbon_taken_once] (Take a ribbon.) -> take
* Not today. -> END

~ take
set ribbon_taken_once
Narrator: She ties it on with cold, quick fingers, and the smile goes out of her eyes for a moment, with relief.
-> daciana

~ who
Daciana [afraid]: I don't know. That's the truth; I don't. I write down who won't wear one, and at dusk I fold the paper under the lamp on Main Street, and in the morning it's gone, and there's a coin there instead.
Daciana: And my brother stays out of the stocks. That's the other half of it. Nobody said it. Nobody had to.
* [Persuasion DC 13] Then stop. We'll see your brother comes to no harm. -> stop_ok | stop_fail
* [Insight DC 12] (Is she telling the whole of it?) -> whole | whole_missed
* We'll see who comes for it. -> watch

~ whole
Narrator: She is. And she's also counting, the way frightened people do: the names on her lists, and the faces in the stocks the next morning. They're the same faces.
-> watch

~ whole_missed
Narrator: A girl with a basket of yellow ribbon and a pencil behind her ear. If she's hiding anything, it's hiding her.
-> watch

~ stop_ok
Daciana: If you're with me. If you're really with me. Then tonight I'll leave the paper blank, and we'll see who's angry about it.
set ribbons_list_blank
-> watch

~ stop_fail
Daciana: You don't know what you're asking. You'll leave in a week. I live here.
-> watch

~ watch
set ribbons_list_known
quest ribbons asked
Daciana: The lamp on Main Street, by the cooper's. At dusk. Please don't make me watch.
-> END

# --- The lamp on Main Street (prop ribbon_lamp) ---

~ lamp
if flag.ribbons_resolved or flag.dobre_beaten or flag.dobre_arrested
Narrator: An ordinary lamp. Nothing under it but mud and somebody's dropped yellow ribbon.
-> END
endif
if not flag.ribbons_list_known
Narrator: A lamp post on Main Street, with a loose stone at its foot that's been lifted a great many times.
-> END
endif
Narrator: A lamp post on Main Street, with a loose stone at its foot. Under it, folded small, a paper of names in a careful round hand.
* Wait for whoever comes for it, after dusk. -> wait
* [if not flag.ribbons_list_forged] (Rewrite the list: names that don't exist.) -> forged_list
* [if flag.ribbons_list_forged and not flag.forged_collected] (Wait for morning, and see if the list is gone.) -> forged_wait
* Leave it. -> END

~ wait
time until 20
Narrator: The lamps come on, one by one, all down the street. The second bell rings. The square empties the way water leaves a basin.
Narrator: Then a man in the Baron's colours comes along the row, smiling the regulation smile, lifts the stone without looking, and pockets the paper. Watchman Dobre.
* [Stealth DC 13] (Follow him and see where it goes.) -> follow | spotted
* Watchman. A word about that paper. -> confront

~ spotted
Narrator: He stops at the corner without turning round.
Watchman Dobre: You can come out. Nobody walks that quietly in Vallaki unless they're up to something, and nobody's allowed to be up to anything. Lads!
-> fight

~ confront
Watchman Dobre: Watch business, friend. Lists of the cheerless, for the Baron's attention. It's the law. Everything's the law, this week.
* [Intimidation DC 14] Whose business, exactly? Say it, or the Baron hears you sell it twice. -> admits | denies
* (Draw steel.) -> fight

~ denies
Watchman Dobre: Twice? I don't know what you mean, and in about one breath neither will you. Lads!
-> fight

~ admits
set ribbons_twist_known
quest ribbons sold_three_times
Watchman Dobre: Twice? Three times, friend, if you must count. One copy to the Baron's clerk; that's the stocks. One to the back door of Wachterhaus; her ladies visit whoever's in the stocks and tell them who put them there. And one to a gentleman on a black horse at the west gate.
Watchman Dobre: The unhappy ones, you see. The castle likes a list of the unhappy. They're the ones who open their doors.
Watchman Dobre [angry]: And now you know, which is a shame for both of us. Lads!
-> fight

~ follow
set ribbons_twist_known
quest ribbons sold_three_times
Narrator: You follow him at a distance. He stops at the mansion's kitchen door, and a clerk takes a copy of the paper without a word.
Narrator: He stops at the back gate of Wachterhaus, and a gloved hand takes another.
Narrator: Then he goes to the west gate, which should be shut, and isn't. A rider on a black horse waits outside it with his hood up. Dobre holds out the last copy.
* (Step out now, before the rider has it.) -> fight_gate
* [if flag.ribbons_list_blank] (Let him hand over a blank page, and watch.) -> blank

~ blank
Narrator: The rider unfolds it, holds it to the lamp, and turns it over. Then he looks at Dobre for a long time, and Dobre stops smiling.
Narrator: The rider rides away without a word. Dobre turns round, and sees you.
Watchman Dobre: You. You did that. Do you know what he does with a man who brings him nothing?
# The rider has gone: Dobre and his lads alone (Wiki finding 17), back on Main Street where their fight stands (finding 78).
Narrator: You don't wait to find out. You're back under the lamp on Main Street when he catches you up, whistling for his lads.
-> fight

~ fight
Narrator: Two watchmen come round the corner at a run, and they aren't smiling either.
combat ribbons_street

~ fight_gate
Narrator: The rider wheels his horse in the gateway and comes back in, drawing a sabre. Dobre's lads come running up the street from the guardhouse.
combat ribbons_gate

# --- After the fight (Daciana comes to the party next morning; the prop at the lamp, or the square) ---

~ after
if flag.ribbons_resolved
-> daciana_after
endif
Daciana [afraid]: I heard the fighting from the bakery. Is it... is it over? Is he...
if not flag.ribbons_twist_known
set ribbons_twist_known
Narrator: In Dobre's satchel there are three copies of tonight's list, not one. Each is marked in the corner in his hand: a crown for the Baron's clerk, a W, and a little black horse.
Daciana: Three. He sold them three times. The Baron, and Lady Wachter, and... who rides a black horse?
interject tag:blunt: Arrigal. The castle's man at the camp. The castle buys lists of who's unhappy.
endif
quest ribbons lists_taken
Narrator: In Dobre's satchel: a bundle of Daciana's lists going back to spring, a purse of old gold with a profile on every coin, and a book he took off somebody before the stocks.
gold +250
give arcane_grimoire_plus_2
Narrator: And three months of names. Every one of them a person who wouldn't smile.
* Give the lists to Father Lucian and Urwin. Let the town see what was done to it. -> to_town
* Take them to the Baron. Dobre can stand in his own stocks. -> to_baron
* Burn them, here, where Daciana can see. -> burn

~ to_town
set ribbons_resolved
set ribbons_ending = "town"
quest ribbons done
Narrator: Lucian reads them at the church door the next morning, slowly, name by name. Urwin stands beside him with his arms folded. By noon the stocks are empty, and nobody in Vallaki wears a ribbon.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +2 thistle +2 liriel_dawnsong +2 wren_featherfoot +1: You gave Vallaki back the names that were sold out from under it
-> END

~ to_baron
set ribbons_resolved
set ribbons_ending = "baron"
quest ribbons done
Narrator: The Baron reads them twice, purple to the ears. "Selling my lists? To that woman? To the castle?" Dobre is in the stocks by noon, in a yellow ribbon, and the Baron sends round a purse "for loyal citizens", which is you.
gold +100
approve kip_smudgewick +1 godrick_pendlebrook -1 thistle -1: You took the lists to the Baron
-> END

~ burn
set ribbons_resolved
set ribbons_ending = "burned"
quest ribbons done
Narrator: Daciana watches the names curl and go, every one, and doesn't say anything until the last of it is ash. Then she takes the ribbon off her own sleeve and drops it in the fire as well.
approve wren_featherfoot +2 liriel_dawnsong +1 thistle +1: You burned Vallaki's lists in front of the girl who wrote them
-> END

~ daciana_after
if flag.ribbons_ending == "town"
Daciana: I sell bread now. Nobody has to wear bread.
elif flag.ribbons_ending == "baron"
Daciana: The Baron says I'm to go on handing out ribbons and leave the counting to him. I don't write anything down any more. I don't think he can read my hand anyway.
else
Daciana: I still give them out, when people ask. Hardly anybody asks.
endif
-> END

# --- A forged list, a sting and a hearing (NPC plan batch 3): the party has Daciana write names that don't exist, watches
# where they turn up, and brings Dobre before the Baron. Two proofs of three win it; otherwise the old fight stands. ---

~ forged_list
set ribbons_list_forged
set ribbons_proofs = 0
quest ribbons forged
Narrator: You dictate the names under Daciana's lamp, and her pencil shakes so badly the lead nearly snaps on 'Zamfir the Glad.'
Narrator: 'Horatiu Cornel' goes down next, followed by 'Mirela Nobody,' each one more improbable than the last.
Daciana [afraid]: No one will believe these are real people, not even Dobre, and when he sees them he will come for my brother.
Narrator: The street lamp gutters in the damp, and the loose stone at its foot waits for the paper.
-> END

~ forged_wait
time until 8
set forged_collected
Narrator: By morning the paper is gone from beneath the loose stone, and a dull copper coin sits in the mud where it lay.
Narrator: Daciana's face is the colour of lard as she ties her shawl with fingers that will not stop trembling.
Daciana [afraid]: They locked someone in the square before dawn, and no one will look closely enough to see who isn't in there.
-> END

# Daciana, once the list is taken (townsfolk:ribbon_girl -> daciana jumps here): where to look.
~ sting
* [if not flag.proof_clerk] [Persuasion DC 12] (Ask the Baron's clerk about Horatiu Cornel.) -> proof_clerk_ok | proof_clerk_miss
* [if not flag.proof_ladies] [Investigation DC 12] (Look at the stocks where Lady Wachter's ladies called.) -> proof_ladies_ok | proof_ladies_miss
* [if not flag.proof_rider] [Stealth DC 12] (Watch the west gate at dusk.) -> proof_rider_ok | proof_rider_miss
* Bring Dobre before the Baron. -> hearing_intro
* Not yet. -> END

~ proof_clerk_ok
set proof_clerk
set ribbons_proofs += 1
time +30
Narrator: The clerk, wearily: "Horatiu Cornel. I have had four watchmen looking for his house since six o'clock, and the street he lives on does not exist."
Narrator: The clerk: "I assumed he was being clever. People in this town are very clever until the stocks are mentioned, and then they remember where they live."
Narrator: His ledger lies open on the blotter, with 'Horatiu Cornel, seditious refusal of yellow' inked in a neat, furious hand.
Narrator: The clerk, nervously: "Watchman Dobre swore he saw the man spit on a marigold. I have already reserved the middle stock, so someone had better be him."
-> sting

~ proof_clerk_miss
time +30
Narrator: The clerk shuts his ledger with a dry snap and rests both ink-stained hands flat upon the leather cover.
Narrator: The clerk, wearily: "The Baron's happiness is a matter of public record, friend, and the Baron's book of names is not."
-> sting

~ proof_ladies_ok
set proof_ladies
set ribbons_proofs += 1
time +30
Narrator: A wicker basket sits before an empty set of stocks, holding a loaf of caraway bread and a folded card for 'Zamfir the Glad.'
Narrator: A townsman, slyly: "Lady Wachter's ladies came by at nine with a teapot and a very improving sermon on fortitude."
Narrator: A townsman: "They read the first two pages to the padlock before the short one noticed there were no feet in the holes."
Narrator: The card is written in violet ink and advises Zamfir that true sunshine is found only in patience and a change of leadership.
-> sting

~ proof_ladies_miss
time +30
Narrator: The stocks stand open and damp in the morning mist, and the townsfolk hurry past with their eyes fixed on their boots.
Narrator: A townsman, nervously: "Nobody visited, nobody stopped, and I have three yards of yellow ribbon on my coat, so good morning to you."
-> sting

~ proof_rider_ok
set proof_rider
set ribbons_proofs += 1
time until 21
Narrator: At dusk a rider on a black horse waits beside the west gate while Dobre holds a lantern up to the folded slip of paper.
Narrator: The rider: "'Mirela Nobody.' You have sold my master a very small joke, watchman, or you cannot read what you steal."
Narrator: Dobre's regulation smile goes stiff and grey around the edges as the horseman stares down at him for a long, quiet minute.
Narrator: A leather purse of heavy castle silver drops into Dobre's palm without another word, and the black horse turns back into the mist.
-> sting

~ proof_rider_miss
time until 21
Narrator: The heavy timbers of the west gate are barred for the night, and the road toward the mountains is empty save for the drifting fog.
Narrator: Whatever business Watchman Dobre keeps beyond the walls, he is not keeping it here this evening.
-> sting

~ hearing_intro
Narrator: A small, nervous crowd gathers by the stocks as you lay your business before the Baron in his coat of forty yellow ribbons.
Baron [smile]: A plot! A municipal plot, right here in the square! How marvellously instructive for the townsfolk. Proceed at once.
Narrator: Watchman Dobre stands beside the platform with his thumbs in his belt, wearing the broad, fixed smile required by town ordinance.
Watchman Dobre [smile]: Happy to assist the inquiry, my lord. Nobody loves an honest accounting more than I do.
Baron: Quite right, Dobre! Now then, tell me all about this Horatiu Cornel and why he isn't wearing his ribbon.
if flag.ribbons_proofs >= 2
-> hearing_won
endif
-> hearing_lost

~ hearing_won
xp fight ribbons_street
set dobre_arrested
set ribbons_twist_known
quest ribbons sold_three_times
Narrator: You lay what you found side by side upon the Baron's bunting-draped table.
Watchman Dobre [afraid]: Wait now, my lord. A watchman hears all sorts of names in the dark, and if a few girls from Lady Wachter's kitchen took a copy...
Narrator: The square goes so quiet you can hear the rain dripping from the noses of the wooden sun-faces overhead.
Baron [angry]: My lists! My good, corrective, municipal lists, peddled to Fiona Wachter's tea-table and the devil on the hill for three sets of silver!
Baron [angry]: That is the least cheerful thing I have ever heard in my life! Put him in the middle stock and tie a double bow on him!
Watchman Dobre [weary]: Mind the collar, lads. It's new wool, and the rain's coming on.
Narrator: Watchman Dobre sits in the middle stock with a soggy yellow ribbon behind his ear and a placard reading 'INSUFFICIENTLY SINCERE' around his neck.
Narrator: He is still smiling the regulation smile, mostly because the Baron's pikeman is standing four paces behind him with a dry stick.
Narrator: On the Baron's table lies the contents of Dobre's satchel: three months of pencilled lists and a small, heavy purse of old Barovian gold.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +2 thistle +1 liriel_dawnsong +2 wren_featherfoot +1: You brought Watchman Dobre before the Baron
-> END

~ hearing_lost
Watchman Dobre [sly]: A loaf of bread and a story about a horse. If that were enough to lock up the watch, half the square would be in timber by supper.
Baron [weary]: Quite so, quite so. All very spirited, I am sure, but a watchman is a watchman and we have six hundred yards of yellow to hang before dusk.
Narrator: Dobre's regulation smile widens by a fraction as he catches the eye of two heavy-shouldered lads by the pump.
Narrator: The crowd melts back into the side streets without looking at you, one hand held carefully over the ribbons on their coats.
-> fight
